WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.290
Boudicca was an ancient Celtic warrior Queen
who lead an army in a rebellion against the

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.290
Roman Empire.

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:09.460
The story of this brave woman’s life has
often been reduced to just a few sentences

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:13.970
in a textbook, and that’s because there
is very little known about her life.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:19.440
There were just two Roman historians; Tacitus
and Cassius Dio, who wrote down information

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:20.440
about Boudicca.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.700
So everything we know about her is skewed
from the enemy’s perspective, and both of

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.520
their versions of the story vary slightly
from the other.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.899
But, even from this biased standpoint, we
can still see her bravery and determination

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:34.280
to protect her people.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:39.519
Despite the fact that we know so little about
her life, she has still made a massive impact,

00:00:39.519 --> 00:00:43.320
and very nearly defeated one of the largest
empires in the world.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:51.140
She is still remembered to this day as being
an epic hero in British history.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:01:01.239
Boudicca, and the Celtic People
The term “Celt” is used to refer to the

00:01:01.239 --> 00:01:06.411
ancient people of the United Kingdom, but
it covers the Gaels, Britons, and Gauls, just

00:01:06.411 --> 00:01:07.411
to name a few.

00:01:07.411 --> 00:01:11.189
There were dozens of different independent
tribes that were not united.

00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:15.100
So, while their lifestyles were often very
similar, they each had their own customs,

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.520
languages, and traditions.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:21.030
So the name “Celt” is just a way for historians
to refer to this ancient society.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:25.580
The Celts did not keep their own written records,
so much of what we know about them comes from

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.200
Roman records and archeological digs.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.729
According to the Romans, Celtic warriors often
fought naked, or, at least, with their shirts

00:01:32.729 --> 00:01:33.729
off.

00:01:33.729 --> 00:01:37.750
Their bodies and faces were painted with intricate
designs, which earned them the nickname “picts”,

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:39.920
which means “the painted people.”

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.350
They were known for being incredibly brutal.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:47.329
During battle, they would begin grunting like
animals, and blew loud horns to startle their

00:01:47.329 --> 00:01:48.329
enemies.

00:01:48.329 --> 00:01:52.250
They beheaded the Romans without hesitation,
because they apparently believed that a man’s

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.920
head contained his power and soul.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.890
Despite their barbaric war strategy, these
were also very intelligent people.

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:03.520
They were the first society to invent chainmail,
which protected them from enemy swords.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.649
Both men and women wore gold necklaces that
included intricate designs.

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:11.300
Paved roads were also a Celtic invention,
even though the Romans later tried to claim

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.830
that they came up with the idea, first.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:18.160
So, in a lot of ways, the Celts were far from
being the bumbling brutes that they are often

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.220
made out to be.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:25.420
Boudicca was the Queen and wife of King Prasutagus,
and they were rulers of the “Iceni” tribe.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.620
They were located in modern-day Norfolk and
Suffolk, and for a while, their people lived

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:30.620
in peace.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:35.580
A Roman statesman and historian named Cassius
Dio describes Boudicca: “In stature she

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:41.030
was very tall, in appearance most terrifying,
in the glance of her eye most fierce, and

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:42.110
her voice was harsh.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:47.100
A great mass of the tawniest hair fell to
her hips; around her neck was a large golden

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:55.700
necklace;
What Boudicca apparently lacked in grace and

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.140
beauty, she made up for in her brilliance.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.940
According to Dio’s backhanded compliment,
she “possessed of greater intelligence than

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.450
often belongs to women”.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.310
In Celtic society, “Druids” were intellectuals
and she wore a tunic of divers colours over

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:09.370
which a thick mantle was fastened with a brooch.”
who held very highly respected positions in

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:10.880
their respective tribes.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.350
They were like priests who performed the pagan
rituals, but they also practiced medicine,

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.540
alchemy, science, and philosophy.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:19.820
And yes, druids could be women, too.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:23.480
Since Boudicca is remembered for being very
smart, it would make sense that she may have

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.650
been a druid in her spare time, apart from
being the Queen.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:29.740
It would have only made a her a more well-respected
leader.

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:33.770
But with the new threat of Roman invasion,
Boudicca would need to focus on protecting

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:35.690
her people more than ever before.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:45.060
The Roman Invasion
The Romans conquered Southern England in 43

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:50.080
AD. King Prasutagus could see that his Celtic
warriors were no match for the Roman Legions,

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.000
so he ordered his warriors to stop their rebellion.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.950
Prasutagus agreed to align with the Roman
Empire for the sake of protecting what remained

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:57.950
of his people.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:00.840
He promised that when he died, his tribe would
be under Roman rule.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:04.790
In his last will and testament, he left his
land to his two daughters, and the Emperor

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:05.790
of Rome.

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:09.480
The King truly did believe that they had made
a peaceful negotiation, and the Romans even

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:14.480
lent the Britons their Roman currency to help
them integrate into their new society.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:19.180
They were given 40,000,000 sesterces, but
they were expected to pay it back over time,

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:20.180
plus interest.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:23.610
There is a debate over how much one sestari
would actually be actually worth in today’s

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.380
money, but the estimates are usually around
$1.50.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:30.570
So their loan was roughly $60 million.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.850
The Britons never asked for a loan in the
first place.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:36.590
In fact, the Celtic tribal people were rich
in resources like gold.

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:40.410
But they accepted the money, anyway, because
it was the only way they could have a currency

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.940
to buy and sell together with the Romans.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:47.220
Nero took Claudius’ place as the new Emperor
of Rome in the year 54.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:51.300
He was just 16 years old at the time, and
he was known for spending an extravagant amount

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:52.300
of money.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:56.720
In turn, he sought to regain much of the lost
funds by going after interest on loans, as

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:57.930
well as taxes.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:02.780
Nero appointed a man named Suetonius Paulinus
to handle the new territory in England.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:07.540
He was given the position of Governor of Britain,
and he was also the General of the Roman army.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:12.031
In the year 60, King Prasutagus died, and
Queen Boudicca became the new leader of the

00:05:12.031 --> 00:05:13.250
Icini tribe.

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:17.310
Of course, the Romans did not see Boudicca
as a legitimate leader, because she was a

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:18.310
woman.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:22.660
As we mentioned earlier, there were contracts
in place before King Prasutagus’ death that

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:24.600
should have guaranteed his people’s safety.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:30.850
But General Paulinus decided to ignore all
of it, and take the Iceni by force.

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:35.050
He used the loan as an excuse, and demanded
that they wanted all 40 million sesterces

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:38.090
to paid back in full, plus interest.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.930
And they needed it all immediately.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:44.889
The Romans went into every single home, taking
anything of value that they could find.

00:05:44.889 --> 00:05:48.100
They captured men as slaves, and raped the
young women.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:50.370
Not even the young princesses were spared.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.300
Boudicca’s daughters were sexually assaulted
by Roman soldiers.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:57.070
When she tried to rescue her daughters, the
soldiers grabbed the Queen, ripped her clothes

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:59.540
off, and beat her bloody in front of her people.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:03.940
Since King Prasutagus truly did leave their
land to the Romans, the Celts could only stand

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:08.300
and watch as their beloved Queen was brutalized
in front of them.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.820
Boudicca had been beaten, but not broken.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:16.010
Instead of withering in self-pity, this attack
only sparked her need for revenge.

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:19.551
Boudicca called a meeting with the leaders
of the local tribes, and learned that they

00:06:19.551 --> 00:06:21.580
had all been treated the same way.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:24.340
Everyone was furious at what the Romans had
done.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:28.440
The leaders of several Celtic tribes all agreed
to put aside their differences and follow

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:30.120
her as their new Queen.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:33.889
It was clear that if the Celts wanted their
freedom, they were going to have to fight

00:06:33.889 --> 00:06:36.200
for it.

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:42.460
Boudicca’s Rebellion
Boudicca had witnessed her husband’s defeat

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:47.690
against the Romans years earlier, and yet,
she was not afraid to fight them once again

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:49.190
for her people’s freedom.

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:54.470
Armed with a sword and her horse-drawn chariot,
Boudicca stood in front of her Celtic warriors.

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:58.450
Just like her men, her face would have been
adored in battle paint.

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:03.460
Women were fighting alongside men, and each
and every one of them had the fire of vengeance

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:04.650
in their hearts.

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:09.080
The Celts began the rebellion by targeting
the Roman city Camulodunum, which is modern-day

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:10.080
Colchester.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:13.580
She waited until General Paulinus was out
of town, and he took his troops with him.

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:17.300
He was on a campaign to capture Anglesey,
which is an island in Wales.

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:21.270
Since he took all of his soldiers with him,
he left Camulodunum virtually undefended.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:25.480
Once he was gone, Boudicca and her men entered
the city, and they slaughtered all of the

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:26.990
Roman men they could find.

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:31.080
When Roman leadership got word that they needed
help defending the city from a tribal Queen

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:35.480
who was leading a rebellion, they grossly
underestimated her, since she was a woman.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:40.020
They sent a measly 200 unarmed slaves to take
care of the issue.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:46.510
Obviously, this handful of men was nothing
compared to her massive army of 120,000 people.

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:50.270
An additional 2,500 Roman soldiers were sent
to help defend the city.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:54.440
For several days, the soldiers tried to keep
civilians alive by hoarding themselves in

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:55.760
the Temple of Claudius.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:00.690
The Celts grew tired of waiting, and set fire
to the temple, which trapped and killed everyone

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:01.690
inside.

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:04.790
When General Paulinus returned to Camulodunum,
he found the temple crumbling in a pile of

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:10.360
ash, as well as the gruesome scene of beheaded
men, dissected bodies, and pools of blood.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:13.800
He knew that his small legion of just 5,000
men would be no match for Boudicca, so he

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:18.600
had no choice but to ride ahead to each major
Roman stronghold, and try to evacuate civilian

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:21.360
refugees before it was too late.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.570
Historian Cassius Dio wrote, “All this ruin
was brought upon the Romans by a woman, a

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:28.730
fact which in itself caused them the greatest
shame.”

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:34.919
Next, the Celts moved on to “Londinium”,
which is modern-day-London, and burned the

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:37.079
entire city to the ground.

00:08:37.079 --> 00:08:41.849
While in London, tribal men who had been enslaved
by the Romans were set free, and they began

00:08:41.849 --> 00:08:42.990
to follow Boudicca.

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:46.250
Her army temporarily grew to over 230,000
troops.

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:49.230
They sacked the city of “Verulamium”,
which is modern-day St. Albans.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:56.079
It is estimated that Boudicca’s army killed
and tortured between 70,000 to 80,000 Roman

00:08:56.079 --> 00:08:57.079
people.

00:08:57.079 --> 00:09:01.040
In the Roman history books, they describe
the Celtic slaughter as being truly barbaric.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:05.149
Some modern-day historians believe that this
imagery was exaggerated in order to demonize

00:09:05.149 --> 00:09:06.220
the Celtic culture.

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:10.959
Just like every other colonizing nation, the
Romans believed that they were the more civilized

00:09:10.959 --> 00:09:15.680
ones, even though they started the fight by
attacking, robbing, and raping the Celtic

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:16.709
people beforehand.

00:09:16.709 --> 00:09:21.920
Boudicca and the rest of the Celts were steadily
making progress to regain the Britons’ independence

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:22.920
from the Roman Empire.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:26.990
At this point, Emperor Nero was seriously
considering pulling his Roman troops out of

00:09:26.990 --> 00:09:27.990
Great Britain.

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:32.240
If Boudicca kept up killing his people at
such a rapid rate, she could topple the entire

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:33.240
empire.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:37.990
And if she had succeeded, she would have gone
down in history as the queen who united the

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:41.700
Celtic tribes under a common cause, and regained
their independence.

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:45.949
But unfortunately for her, General Paulinus
was confident that he could win.

00:09:45.949 --> 00:09:50.540
He convinced Emperor Nero to stay, because
he was ready with a strategy to take down

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:59.699
the resistance once and for all.

00:09:59.699 --> 00:10:01.829
The Battle of Watling Street
The final confrontation was known as The Battle

00:10:01.829 --> 00:10:02.920
of Watling Street.

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:07.019
As the name suggests, the two armies met at
a Roman road called “Watling Street.”

00:10:07.019 --> 00:10:11.439
While there is a map of this road, most historians
disagree over the exact location, because

00:10:11.439 --> 00:10:15.519
there is only given a brief description of
the location given by the historian Tacitus.

00:10:15.519 --> 00:10:19.499
General Paulinus chose this location because
it was ideal for his defensive strategy.

00:10:19.499 --> 00:10:24.220
A forest protected the back of the Roman soldiers,
so they could not be attacked from behind.

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:29.189
There was an open field in front of the forest,
which forced Boudicca’s army to charge straight

00:10:29.189 --> 00:10:30.189
towards them.

00:10:30.189 --> 00:10:33.490
As mentioned earlier, General Paulinus only
had 5,000 soldiers in his army.

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:37.860
He had to send letters to all of the veterans
living in Great Britain who were much older

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:39.370
and enjoying their retirement.

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:43.860
Luckily for him, an additional 5,000 men were
still loyal to the Empire, and he was able

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.509
to assemble a total of 10,000 troops.

00:10:46.509 --> 00:10:49.680
Even after calling for backup, he was outnumbered
by Boudicca’s army.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:53.639
According to the Roman historian Cassius Dio,
Queen Boudicca spent time before the battle

00:10:53.639 --> 00:10:58.470
giving a long and riveting speech about fighting
for freedom that sounds like it’s straight

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:04.119
out of a Hollywood movie: "If you weigh well
the strengths of our armies you will see that

00:11:04.119 --> 00:11:07.029
in this battle we must conquer or die.

00:11:07.029 --> 00:11:08.809
This is a woman's resolve.

00:11:08.809 --> 00:11:13.910
As for the men, they may live or be slaves...I
am not fighting for my kingdom and wealth

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:14.910
now.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:19.819
I am fighting as an ordinary person for my
lost freedom, my bruised body, and my outraged

00:11:19.819 --> 00:11:20.819
daughters."

00:11:20.819 --> 00:11:25.850
However, Dio wasn’t even there to listen,
and there were very few witnesses who actually

00:11:25.850 --> 00:11:26.850
survived.

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:32.420
So we can’t be sure if a speech was said
at all...Especially since she spends a lot

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:35.680
of time trying to convince her troops that
even though she’s “just” a woman, they

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:37.290
should still trust her, anyway.

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:40.589
If you think about it, a hundred thousand
Celts wouldn’t be there if they didn’t

00:11:40.589 --> 00:11:42.089
trust her in the first place.

00:11:42.089 --> 00:11:46.249
And archeological evidence points to proof
that the Celts gave women powerful military

00:11:46.249 --> 00:11:47.740
positions for hundreds of years, already.

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:49.449
But we’ll get to that later.

00:11:49.449 --> 00:11:54.720
The Celts had plenty of weapons and supplies
that they had scavenged from the Roman cities

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:59.260
they defeated, but they were still not as
well-equipped as the enemy.

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:03.790
Roman soldiers wore full suits of armor, helmets,
and had sandals with nails at the bottom to

00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:05.689
help them from slipping in the grass.

00:12:05.689 --> 00:12:08.089
They also had swords, and large shields.

00:12:08.089 --> 00:12:12.509
Their spears could be thrown long distances,
which helped them avoid hand-to-hand combat.

00:12:12.509 --> 00:12:16.330
Despite these setbacks, the Celtic people
were so confident that they would win the

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:20.180
battle, they even brought a wagon train with
their wives and children.

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:24.489
The wagons formed a semi-circle around the
back of the Celtic army, sealing off the exit.

00:12:24.489 --> 00:12:28.910
According to records, General Paulinus could
see the fear in his men’s eyes as they started

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:31.759
at this army that was ten times larger than
theirs.

00:12:31.759 --> 00:12:35.100
But he used insults to snap the fear out of
his men.

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:37.670
He tried to boost their morale by mocking
the enemy.

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:41.380
He said that the Celts were savages, and pointed
to the female warriors as evidence that they

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:43.290
were weaker than the Romans.

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:47.459
Just like he did with Boudicca, the historian
Cassius Dio invented yet another long and

00:12:47.459 --> 00:12:51.079
grandiose speech for General Paulinus to say
to his troops.

00:12:51.079 --> 00:12:55.379
While we may not be sure exactly what words
were exchanged, we do know what the general’s

00:12:55.379 --> 00:12:56.679
battle strategy was.

00:12:56.679 --> 00:13:00.879
He knew that even though his army had fewer
men, they were at a great advantage with their

00:13:00.879 --> 00:13:01.879
technology.

00:13:01.879 --> 00:13:05.160
The Roman soldiers stood side-by-side and
crouched together behind their tall shields.

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:08.629
During the first round of the battle, the
Celts charged towards the Romans, who threw

00:13:08.629 --> 00:13:10.470
their spears into the air.

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:14.079
Without heavy metal armor to protect them,
the spears went through the chainmail and

00:13:14.079 --> 00:13:16.350
bare chests of the Celtic warriors.

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:19.670
Boudicca told the men to fall back before
pressing on once more.

00:13:19.670 --> 00:13:24.059
When the Celts pushed up against this wall
of shields, the Romans would draw their swords,

00:13:24.059 --> 00:13:25.980
and stab the men in the chest.

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:29.910
Every few minutes, the Roman soldiers would
rotate, and the man who was just fighting

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:31.510
moved to the back of the line.

00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:36.190
This way, each man was fresh and ready for
battle, and it gave everyone a chance to rest.

00:13:36.190 --> 00:13:39.939
After the Celts had exhausted thousands of
their soldiers, General Paulinus gave the

00:13:39.939 --> 00:13:44.700
order for his men to push themselves together
into a triangular formation, as their charged

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:46.029
towards the Celtic army.

00:13:46.029 --> 00:13:49.549
This was an incredibly powerful metallic force
that pushed against the men, and it forced

00:13:49.549 --> 00:13:51.199
the Celtic people to retreat.

00:13:51.199 --> 00:13:55.379
Cassius Dio wrote, “The remainder took to
flight, although escape was difficult, as

00:13:55.379 --> 00:13:58.119
the cordon of wagons had blocked the outlets.

00:13:58.119 --> 00:14:03.110
The troops gave no quarter, even to the women:
the baggage animals themselves had been speared

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:05.179
and added to the pile of bodies.”

00:14:05.179 --> 00:14:09.649
According to Roman records, 80,000 Celtic
men, women, and children were killed during

00:14:09.649 --> 00:14:17.759
The Battle of Watling Street, and just 400
Romans died at the hands of Boudicca’s army.

00:14:17.759 --> 00:14:23.570
Death and Legacy
After losing the battle, records say that

00:14:23.570 --> 00:14:24.769
Boudicca managed to run.

00:14:24.769 --> 00:14:28.410
While we do not know exactly what happened
next, Tacitus speculated that she would have

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:31.839
poisoned herself, because it would have been
the honorable thing to do at the time.

00:14:31.839 --> 00:14:35.309
However, Cassius Dio wrote that she later
died of an illness.

00:14:35.309 --> 00:14:38.429
Ultimately, we do not know when, or how she
died.

00:14:38.429 --> 00:14:40.589
For years, Boudicca’s story was all but
forgotten.

00:14:40.589 --> 00:14:44.470
Since this was such an embarrassing stain
in the Roman Empire’s history, it only makes

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:46.679
sense that it was buried for a long time.

00:14:46.679 --> 00:14:50.420
But during the Renaissance, the story of the
Celtic Queen’s bravery began to emerge once

00:14:50.420 --> 00:14:51.420
again.

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:55.910
She was remembered and painted by artists,
and during the Victorian Era, statues were

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:56.910
created in her honor.

00:14:56.910 --> 00:15:00.059
It didn’t take long before she became a
British icon.

00:15:00.059 --> 00:15:04.369
Despite the detailed Roman records, some people
began to wonder if she ever existed at all,

00:15:04.369 --> 00:15:08.769
and considered the possibility that this warrior
Queen may actually be a legend.

00:15:08.769 --> 00:15:12.620
In fact, even the named “boudīkā” comes
from the Celtic word meaning “victorious”.

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:16.929
At the very least, there is a chance that
Boudicca may not have been her real name,

00:15:16.929 --> 00:15:20.489
and it was just a placeholder put in place
of her true identity.

00:15:20.489 --> 00:15:24.980
It was in the Victorian era that Boudica's
fame took on legendary proportions as Queen

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:30.499
Victoria came to be seen as Boudica's "namesake",
their names being identical in meaning.

00:15:30.499 --> 00:15:34.370
Victoria's Poet Laureate, Alfred, Lord Tennyson,
wrote a poem, "Boadicea", and several ships

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:35.420
were named after her.

00:15:35.420 --> 00:15:39.809
In 2001, archeologists uncovered a burial
site of a Celtic female warrior who was laid

00:15:39.809 --> 00:15:41.779
to rest with a huge amount of respect.

00:15:41.779 --> 00:15:46.220
At her feet were small treasures of a mirror,
a brooch, and blue glass beads.

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:49.649
Her chariot was decorated with red coral gathered
from the sea.

00:15:49.649 --> 00:15:53.069
Two other soldiers were buried next to this
woman and her chariot.

00:15:53.069 --> 00:15:57.389
These are known as The Wetwang Graves, and
all of the artifacts from the archaeological

00:15:57.389 --> 00:15:59.970
site are now kept at the British Museum.

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:03.439
According to Tony Spence, who works at the
Department of Prehistory &amp; Europe for the

00:16:03.439 --> 00:16:07.569
British Museum, they believe that this woman
was a Queen, because of the special care and

00:16:07.569 --> 00:16:09.399
respect that was given to her remains.

00:16:09.399 --> 00:16:13.540
Her chariot and the other objects surrounding
her were also so expensive, only royalty could

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:15.500
afford such an extravagant burial.

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:18.639
She would have been in her late 30’s to
early 40’s, and she was very tall.

00:16:18.639 --> 00:16:22.410
They were able to use her skull to do a facial
reconstruction of what she would have looked

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:23.410
look like.

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:27.199
Her face was disfigured, so it’s easy to
see how she could be described as “frightening”,

00:16:27.199 --> 00:16:30.610
like how Cassius Dio described Boudicca.

00:16:30.610 --> 00:16:34.290
Despite the fact that nearly every detail
seems to line up with the story of Boudicca,

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:39.010
carbon dating done by Oxford University in
2017 placed these artifacts anywhere between

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:44.731
the years 145 to 200 BC, which would have
been 200-300 years before Boudicca’s battle

00:16:44.731 --> 00:16:45.731
took place.

00:16:45.731 --> 00:16:49.309
But scientists are now saying the carbon dating
has the potential to be wrong.

00:16:49.309 --> 00:16:53.959
In 2018, Cornell University did an experiment
which showed proof of carbon dating that was

00:16:53.959 --> 00:16:56.019
offset by several hundred years.

00:16:56.019 --> 00:17:00.420
This means that many of the historic timelines
we have been taught about ancient civilizations

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:02.540
could actually be wrong.

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:07.550
Without a written record or a time machine,
we may never know the full story behind the

00:17:07.550 --> 00:17:14.140
true identities of those people left in these
graves, and it may remain an unsolved mystery

00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:15.140
forever.

00:17:15.140 --> 00:17:19.699
But whether the Wetwang grave belongs to Boudicca
or not, it still proves, undoubtedly, that

00:17:19.699 --> 00:17:22.539
the Celts included warrior women in battle.

00:17:22.539 --> 00:17:26.500
And if they truly are 200 years before her
time, it only goes to show that in ancient

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:31.060
Briton, women were respected enough to lead
armies for several generations.

00:17:31.060 --> 00:17:35.690
The remains of this warrior queen may sit
in plastic bags and cardboard boxes in a museum,

00:17:35.690 --> 00:17:40.380
but the spirit of these leaders are still
very much alive in the hearts of everyone

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:42.490
who stands up against their oppressors.

00:17:42.490 --> 00:18:01.710
Even if she was on the losing side of the
war, Boudicca truly was

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:04.869
a hero.

